module gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_scope

import std.types { Bool, String, List }

// WHAT THE DEPLOY ACTUALLY INSTALLS, DECLARED ONCE BECAUSE TWO THINGS NEED IT AND MUST NOT DISAGREE.
// The working-tree leg of the source sync rsyncs the runner checkout wholesale except these
// prefixes, so the deployed source is the WORKING TREE, not the commit. Two consumers read it:
//
//   gunbc.live_deploy.emit       renders `--exclude /<p>` per prefix into the tree-sync ExecStart
//   gunbc.live_deploy.candidate  decides whether an untracked path would actually be shipped
//
// Spelling it in both would let them drift, in the dangerous direction: admission would pass on a
// path the sync then installs. Same fact, one home (DESIGN §3).
//
// IT LIVES IN ITS OWN LEAF MODULE FOR A STRUCTURAL REASON. Putting it beside the emitter and
// importing that from candidate closes a real cycle — candidate is reached from
// gunbc.hostname_read, which gunbc.host_effect reaches, which the emitter's own closure reaches —
// and acyclicity is the import graph's one structural law (DESIGN §3). A fact needed by two modules
// on opposite sides of a dependency chain belongs beneath both, not inside either.
// WHY THIS LIST IS NOT JUST target AND .git, AND WHY IT IS STILL NOT EVERY IGNORED PATH.
// The working-tree leg is rsync, which does not read .gitignore, so an ignored path outside these
// prefixes is copied to the host under a revision that does not name it. Candidate admission
// refuses on exactly that state (gunbc.live_deploy.candidate, the ignored_deployed arm), naming two
// remedies: widen these exclusions, or remove the file. The four appended here take the first
// because they are host state by construction -- agent session state and local tool/build caches
// the deployed tree must never contain under any revision, so shipping one is never the intent and
// refusing on one is pure friction.
//
// THE .env FAMILY IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT AND STAYS REFUSED. The repository ignores `.env`,
// `.env.local` and `.env.*.local`; the third is a wildcard, and both this predicate and rsync's
// `--exclude /<p>` match a literal segment. Listing the two spellable forms would silence admission
// on them while `.env.*.local` kept shipping -- partial cover on the one class where the shipped
// bytes are secrets, strictly worse than the present loud refusal on all three. It stays refused
// until the exclusion carrier can express a pattern. src/v2/test/claim/manual/sg2_scratch_probe.dag
// stays refused for a different reason: it sits inside a source root the graph actually reads, so
// excluding it would change what the host compiles rather than merely what it stores.
//
// NOT DERIVED FROM gunbc.gitignore_authority, ON PURPOSE. That authority answers "must not be
// tracked"; this one answers "must not be shipped", and the sets differ -- a tracked file ships and
// is not ignored. Deriving would also let an ordinary .gitignore edit silently change what a deploy
// installs, and would put an import edge into a module kept leaf for the acyclicity reason above.
// THE TWO BOOTSTRAP RECEIPT CHANNELS ARE EXCLUDED FOR THE SAME REASON `.gunbc` IS: they are
// per-invocation runtime state the launcher writes under the cwd, and the cwd of `gunbc converge
// --mode deploy` IS the deploying checkout. The first deploy through that verb refused itself --
// CandidateCheckoutNotClean, ignored-deployed=.gunbc-converge-receipts/receipt-446101-0 -- because
// the verb's own receipt from the attempt before sat in a shipped path. Excluding the channel makes
// the verb's residue invisible to the candidate it produces, which is the only consistent answer:
// a receipt of the deploy is never part of what the deploy installs.
data deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes: List<String> = [
  "target", ".git", ".claude", ".gunbc", "target-codex", ".cargo-home",
  ".gunbc-build-receipts", ".gunbc-converge-receipts",
]

fn deployed_tree_rsync_exclude_flags() -> String {
  join(map(deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes, p => concat("--exclude /", p)), " ")
}

// Segment-wise, never a character run: `targetish/x` starts with `target` but is not under it, and treating
// it as excluded would hide from admission a path rsync goes on to ship.
fn path_is_deployed(path: String) -> Bool {
  !any(deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes, p => path == p || starts_with(s: path, prefix: concat(p, "/")))
}

// WHICH REPOSITORY TRANSITION THIS DEPLOYMENT IS BOUND TO, AND WHY IT IS A WALL RATHER THAN A NOTE.
//
// The `.git` leg of the source sync copies `HEAD`, `objects`, `packed-refs` and `refs` as FILES and
// excludes `index` entirely, so the target receives working files from one revision and git
// metadata from another. Measured on srv1 2026-08-27, `/opt/gunbc/gunbc` is in three-way
// divergence -- `git status --porcelain` reports 5650 paths, of which 1964 are
// tracked-and-staged-present but absent from the worktree and 1731 are present but unknown to
// HEAD -- with the first status column (index vs HEAD) and the second (worktree vs index) BOTH
// wrong. That is one deployment's output, not two racing: no concurrency control can make a
// single execution of this operation valid.
//
// THIS EXISTS BECAUSE A PR COMMENT IS NOT A WALL. The change that restored the dashboard deploy
// mode recorded, in prose, that the mode must not be dispatched until this leg is replaced. DESIGN
// section 5: construction beats validation and a state you can still write will be written; an
// operator memory and a PR body are neither. So the unsafe dispatch is refused HERE, before the
// launcher observes anything, and the prose is demoted to explanation.
//
// ITS RUNG, HONESTLY: mechanically preventable, not structural. The wall executes and refuses on
// the real path, but the bound realization is a HAND-DECLARED row -- someone could edit it without
// having done the work, and nothing would notice. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: derive the bound realization
// from the emitted tree-sync unit itself, so the declaration cannot disagree with what is installed.
//
// DISSOLVES ON: the Git-native transition landing, at which point this row becomes
// `GitNativeConvergence`, the refusal arm stops being reachable from production, and its
// discriminating control is retained as a regression probe rather than deleted (section 4b(4)).
type RepositoryTransitionRealization
  = LegacyGitFileSync
  | GitNativeConvergence

data deployed_tree_repository_transition: RepositoryTransitionRealization = GitNativeConvergence

type RepositoryTransitionAdmission
  = RepositoryTransitionAdmitted
  | LegacyGitFileSyncNotAdmitted { reason: String }

// THE REFUSAL NAMES THE MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN ASSERTING A JUDGEMENT, so an operator who hits it can
// check the claim on the host instead of taking this module's word for it.
fn repository_transition_admission(
  realization: RepositoryTransitionRealization,
) -> RepositoryTransitionAdmission {
  match realization {
    GitNativeConvergence => RepositoryTransitionAdmitted
    LegacyGitFileSync =>
      LegacyGitFileSyncNotAdmitted {
        reason: "the deployed-tree sync still transports .git as files (HEAD/objects/packed-refs/refs copied, index excluded), which leaves the target with working files and git metadata from different revisions -- measured on srv1 as 5650 divergent paths with index-vs-HEAD and worktree-vs-index both wrong. One execution of this operation cannot produce a valid target state, so no dispatch is admitted until the Git-native transition replaces it",
      }
  }
}
